WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:05.280
Before any meaningful introduction&nbsp;
into the history of the slavic slave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.160
trade can be conducted first we must address the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.200
etymology of the word slave. But even before&nbsp;
that this video was made possible thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.420
my Patreons and YouTube members. If you want&nbsp;
to support what I do it would be tremendously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.100
appreciated if you checked my Patreon or&nbsp;
YouTube membership. Now back to the video.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.580
Latin had multiple words that could mean slave,&nbsp;
like mancipium and ancilla, but the most common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:36.360
latin word for slave was servus or servi in&nbsp;
plural. This word survived in usage even after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.880
the fall of the Western Roman Empire because Latin&nbsp;
still largely remained the governing language of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.520
both the state and the church. Of course, some&nbsp;
new Germanic words for slaves were sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:54.060
used interchangeably with the latin ones like þeow&nbsp;
in old English or þræll in old Norse, but largely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.040
in most written documents, the latin word&nbsp;
of servus remained the most dominant word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.980
for slave in early medieval Europe. However,&nbsp;
this dominance of the word servus at a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.600
when slavery lacked the standardization&nbsp;
and uniformity of the old Roman system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.700
meant that the word began to be increasingly&nbsp;
ambiguous in its definition. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.120
there are a lot of different types of servitude&nbsp;
and the word servus, ever since the 5th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.760
was increasingly ambiguous about the type&nbsp;
of servitude it was referring to. As Alice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:32.700
Rio states ‘it is almost impossible to tell&nbsp;
what exactly the word servus was intended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.560
describe when it is found in an early mediaeval&nbsp;
text without further contextual information.’

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.620
For example, when the law books of King&nbsp;
Stephen mention some servi in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.960
of a castle these are most certainly&nbsp;
‘royal servants’, not slaves. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.900
when in the same time period, King Ladislaus&nbsp;
orders that the escaped servi be captured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.240
punished, and returned to their masters, he was&nbsp;
most definitely referring to slaves not royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.700
servants. To add even more confusion, during&nbsp;
the early to high middle ages a linguistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.520
shift occurred where the word servus started&nbsp;
to increasingly mean serf rather than slave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.720
and by the late middle ages servus was&nbsp;
almost exclusively used to refer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:22.260
serfs not slaves. In fact, the modern English&nbsp;
word serf comes from the latin word servus.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.720
This changing of the meaning of the word servus,&nbsp;
over the course of the middle ages, presented a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:33.120
problem, because, even though yes certain kind&nbsp;
of feudal serfdoms could resemble slavery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:39.840
serfdom in practice, and in medieval legal&nbsp;
terms, was never actual slavery. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:46.320
the word servus could no longer be used to mean&nbsp;
slave and so, a new word had to be adopted. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.000
were several candidates for this new word. For&nbsp;
example, the latin word for a ‘captured person’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.660
captivus was sometimes used in the middle ages as&nbsp;
the word for a slave, either in combination with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.000
the word servus or on its own. The previously&nbsp;
mentioned other latin words which could mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.160
slave where also sometimes used. However, what&nbsp;
eventually became the most common type of word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.380
to define a slave was not somekind of a&nbsp;
latin word but an ethnonym. To explain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:20.700
enslaved people were often recorded as captivus&nbsp;
or servus or both plus an ethnonym. e.g.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:27.060
servus captivus wealh or captivus sardus.&nbsp;
After a while, especially if the slaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.080
being captured and sold in a particular&nbsp;
area were regularly the same ethnicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:38.040
these written recordes became abbreviated to just&nbsp;
the ethnonym. So, in areas like in early Medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.280
England, where most of the slaves captured were&nbsp;
Welsh, the latin word for welsh, wealh, became,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.860
for a bit, synonymous with the word slave.&nbsp;
Same thing happened in medieval Genoa with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:55.320
the ethnonym sardus, meaning sardinian, and with&nbsp;
many other ethnonyms in other slave trading areas.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.060
However, there was one ethnonym to rule them&nbsp;
all, one ethnonym that would not only become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:06.120
synonymous with the word slave … it is where&nbsp;
the word slave comes from, and that ethnonym&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:13.800
is … slav, or Sclavus in medieval latin or&nbsp;
Σκλάβος (sclavos) in Byznatine Greek. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.360
to understand how the slavic ethnonym became&nbsp;
the foundation for almost every western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.640
language’s word for slave, we must talk about&nbsp;
the history of the medieval Slavic slave trade.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.320
…

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:30.600
The first undisputed appearance of the Slavs in&nbsp;
the written record was in the mid 6th century on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.010
the Byzantine northern border. The Byzantines&nbsp;
wrote that these people were called Σκλαβηνοί&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:38.400
(Sclavenoi), or Σκλαβοί when shortened (Sclavoi&nbsp;
(second declension plural)) or, when Latinized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.380
Sclaveni, or Sclavi when shortened. These&nbsp;
latin and greek names for the Slavs were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:48.120
obviously derived from the Slavic endonym&nbsp;
(Slověně) which either came from the slavic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.820
word ‘slovo’ meaning word or ‘slava’ meaning&nbsp;
glory. There were slight variations in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:59.040
the slavic ethnonym was written in early mediaeval&nbsp;
texts but over the course of the middle ages the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.860
shortened versions of the name (that is Σκλαβοί&nbsp;
in Greek and Sclavi in Latin) became the standard.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.780
The early Slavs of the 6th century proved&nbsp;
increasingly bothersome for the Byzantines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:15.180
mainly because the Slavs weren’t unified, which&nbsp;
meant it was really hard for the Byzantines to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:20.400
control them. If Byzantium made peace with one&nbsp;
tribe, there were 10 other tribes that it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.100
apply to. As a result of this disjointed nature of&nbsp;
the Slavs, there were some Slavic tribes that held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.420
frequent independent raids into the Byzantine&nbsp;
Balkan territories while other Slavic tribes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:35.100
settled in those territories. Some tribes allied&nbsp;
themselves with the various central European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:39.900
peoples like the Cutrigurs, the Gepids, and the&nbsp;
Lombards and fought with them in frequent wars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.640
All while some Slavic tribes allied themselves&nbsp;
with the Byzantines and fought alongside them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.560
and some entrepreneurial slavs simply fought&nbsp;
as mercenaries for anyone who would hire them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:54.540
This disjointed nature of the slavs, combined&nbsp;
with the 6th century political instability of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.340
central Europe, resulted in a structure in&nbsp;
which slavs could be easily captured through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:04.080
frequent wars and raids and sold into the&nbsp;
mediterranean slave markets which were still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.300
alive even after the fall of the Western Roman&nbsp;
Empire. Capturing slaves in wars and raids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.500
however, wasn’t a new thing nor was&nbsp;
it initially more common in central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.580
and eastern Europe than in other parts of&nbsp;
Europe. The Byzantines and Sassanids often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:22.440
enslaved each other's people during war, as&nbsp;
did the various post Roman Germanic kingdoms.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.300
With that said, between the 7th to&nbsp;
the 8th centuries there was a steady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.380
increase in written documents mentioning&nbsp;
slavic slaves. To give some examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:36.540
there is a 7th century account of Justinian&nbsp;
II selling Slavic slaves in Anatolia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:41.100
there is an 8th century mention of&nbsp;
slavic slaves being sold in Thessaloniky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:47.400
there is another 8th century mention of Bavarian&nbsp;
slave raids into Carinthia, today’s slovenia, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:52.560
there is also a particularly interesting mention&nbsp;
of a Slavic female slave in the Freising Diocese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.600
called Saška the Slav (Sasca Sclauam). However,&nbsp;
this steady increase of Slavic slaves in the 7th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.420
to 8th centuries was nothing compared to the 9th&nbsp;
and 10th centuries’ explosion of slavic slavery.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.700
This explosion of Slavic slavery in the 9th&nbsp;
and 10th century can be attributed to multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:13.860
factors. The first and most important factor&nbsp;
was the establishment of the quite rich Islamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.680
Caliphate which had a huge demand for slaves.&nbsp;
Second, unlike in the south or west, political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.840
power in eastern europe was still very disjointed,&nbsp;
meaning it was much easier to raid the numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:30.600
slavic tribes and polities for slaves than more&nbsp;
established larger kingdoms. Third, the newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:36.120
consolidated Frankish Empire, even though largely&nbsp;
moving away from slavery in favor of serfdom, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.920
in a perfict position to not necessarily use the&nbsp;
slaves they captured on their eastern frontier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:46.680
but sell them into the Medditeranea Slave markets.&nbsp;
Fourth, considering that by this point all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:52.560
major religions ‘forbade the enslavement of people&nbsp;
adhering to the same faith’, the still largely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:58.620
pagan slavs proved to be the perfect source&nbsp;
of pagan slaves. Lastly fifth, the emergence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:03.900
of the ‘viking raiding and trading network’ in&nbsp;
the 9th century opened up new trade routes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.940
eastern Europe through which Slavic slaves could&nbsp;
be more easely captured and sold to Byzantium or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:14.640
the Caliphate. And so, due to all these reasons,&nbsp;
the slavic slave trade blew up in the 9th and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.100
10th centuries as the lands of Eastern Europe&nbsp;
proved to be the perfect source of new slaves.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:24.720
Due to this boom in slavic slavery&nbsp;
new trade routes developed that began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.900
in Eastern Europe and flowed into&nbsp;
the markets of the Mediterranean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:32.520
Black, and Caspian seas. These trade&nbsp;
routes could initially be divided into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:37.260
two major areas of operation. The western&nbsp;
trade routes and the eastern trade routes.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:41.820
In the east the slavic slave trade&nbsp;
was largely dictated by vikings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:47.160
known as Varangians in eastern europe, the&nbsp;
Byzantense, and Nomadic polities like the Khazars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:52.560
Volga Bulgars, and Hungarians. The majority of&nbsp;
slaves from eastern Europe were sold to to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.900
Islamic Caliphate. It should, therefore, not be&nbsp;
surprising that many of the written records we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:03.780
have about these eastern trade routes come from&nbsp;
Arab explorers and geographers like Ibn Fadlan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:09.420
or Ibn Rustah, the latter of whom stated that
‘The Varangians … raid the Saqaliba (the Slavs),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.500
sailing in their ships until they come&nbsp;
upon them, take them captive and sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:18.720
them in Khazaria and in Bulgaria. They have no&nbsp;
cultivated fields and they live by pillaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.980
the land of the Saqaliba (the Slavs) … They&nbsp;
have no dwellings, villages or cultivated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.440
fields. They earn their living by trading … They&nbsp;
treat their slaves well and dress them suitably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:33.420
because for them they are an article of trade.’
Ibn Rusta also mentions the Hungarians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.740
or in other words the Magyars, while they&nbsp;
were still living in the pontic steppe.&nbsp;

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.480
‘They [the Magyars] are lords over all the&nbsp;
Saqaliba (Slavs) who neighbour them and impose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:47.160
a heavy tribute on them. These Saqaliba&nbsp;
(Slavs) are completely at their mercy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:52.200
like prisoners … They make piratical raids on&nbsp;
the Saqaliba (Slavs) and then follow the coast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.420
of the Black Sea with their captives to a port in&nbsp;
Byzantine territory named Kerch (in Crimea) … When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:02.880
the Magyars bring their prisoners to Kerch, the&nbsp;
Greeks go there to trade. The Magyars sell their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.040
Saqaliba slaves and buy Byzantine brocade, woollen&nbsp;
rugs and other products of the Byzantine empire.’

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.140
Slave raiding parties of Khazars,&nbsp;
Bulgars, Hungarians, Varangians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:18.120
and rival Slavic tribes where a common place&nbsp;
in medieval eastern slavic lives. There where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.040
even mentions of slavic raiding parties along&nbsp;
the danube river which sold slavic slaves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:29.160
Byzantium. The Byzantines also often enslaved&nbsp;
the Slavs living on their northern border. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.500
Youval Rotman states, for the Byzantines ‘the&nbsp;
taking of captives and their enslavement seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.980
to have been the general rule in the&nbsp;
Balkans until the eleventh century.’

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:44.340
Switching now over to the western trade routes.&nbsp;
They began on the Frankish and Moravian eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:49.500
frontiers and flowed into the trade ports of&nbsp;
Venice, Marseilles, and overland to Verdun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.960
often eventually ending up in Cordova.
Arab geographer Ibn Hawqal, tells us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:00.780
that in the 10th century all of the slaves in&nbsp;
al-Andalus, todays Spain, came from the ‘western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:06.180
lands of the Saqaliba’. For they are being raided&nbsp;
from one side by nomadic tribes and from the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:12.000
by the Franks. He concludes with ‘In these&nbsp;
areas, many captives can still be obtained’.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:18.060
In fact, so many captives can still be obtained&nbsp;
from this area that Prague became one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:24.180
well known European slave markets of the 10th&nbsp;
century. Ibrahim ibn Ya‘qub visited Prague in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:29.460
the 10th century, most likely on a trading trip,&nbsp;
and described the city thusly. ‘The city of Prague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:34.140
is built of stone and lime and it is the most&nbsp;
important trading-site of this country … The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:38.460
Norse and the Saqaliba (the slavs) come there&nbsp;
from Kraków with commodities to trade, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:43.980
do Muslim merchants from the lands of the Turks,&nbsp;
as well as Hungarians and Jews, all with gold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:49.500
They carry away slaves, tin and various kinds&nbsp;
of furs. Their country is the best in the north’

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:55.200
Just like in the east, slave raiding expeditions&nbsp;
into the western Slavic lands where quite common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:01.380
Franks raided the Polebian Slavs, Moravians&nbsp;
and later Bohemians raided Silesians and Poles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.120
and Bavarians raided the Carinthians,&nbsp;
today’s Slovenia. Venice also frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.520
raided the adriatic coast for slavic slaves.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.740
The slavic slaves captured by the Bohemians&nbsp;
and the Franks where mostly sent to Verdun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:16.800
which was a major medieval slave hub where many of&nbsp;
the Slavic Slaves where castrated. This was mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.140
done to prepare them for the islamic market which&nbsp;
preferred eunuchs. As one muslim writer put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:27.360
‘If there are two Slavic brothers from the same&nbsp;
mother and father, even if one of them is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:32.400
twin brother of the other, when one of them&nbsp;
is castrated, he becomes a better servant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.740
smarter in all kinds of activity and manual work.&nbsp;
He will be more skilled in them and more fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.620
for them. You will also find him more intelligent&nbsp;
in conversation – these are all his qualities.’

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:48.600
The volume of primarily Slavic slaves,&nbsp;
but not just, being traded through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.460
western Europe to the primarily Muslim but&nbsp;
also the Byzantine worlds, was so large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:59.400
that according to Michael McCormick this trade&nbsp;
was the Origins of the European Economy well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:04.980
more precisely, the origin of the western&nbsp;
european economy. McCormick argues that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:10.320
export of slaves played a primary, but not&nbsp;
exclusive, role in securing the privileged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:16.620
economic position of Western Europe going into the&nbsp;
late middle ages and beyond. The fact the west did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:22.020
not use a lot of slaves in the middle ages but&nbsp;
traded them for what he calls ‘southern riches’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:27.300
created a massive trade surplus for western&nbsp;
Europe which paved the way for later Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:32.760
European economic expansion. Now this theory&nbsp;
is currently being debated, but the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:38.280
it can even be seriously proposed showcases&nbsp;
the scale of the medieval Slavic slave trade.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:43.440
Due to this large scale slavic slave trade,&nbsp;
it should not be suprising that the slavic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:49.860
ethnonym became to be synonymous with slavery. The&nbsp;
fact that the majority of slaves being traded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:55.380
the European and Islamic markets where Slavs&nbsp;
meant that the latin word for Slav, sclavus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.380 --> 00:14:01.200
became first synonyms with the word servus,&nbsp;
and then, when servus became to mean serf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:06.780
sclavus simply took its place and gradually,&nbsp;
over the course of the 9th to 11th centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:12.480
became the default word for slave. To quote&nbsp;
William Phillips ‘To describe the true slaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.820
a new word was coined, derived from the most&nbsp;
numerous ethnic group in the medieval slave trade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:24.360
the Slavs. The word has cognates in all Western&nbsp;
languages: slave in English, escalve in French,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:29.940
esclavo in Spanish, escravo in Portuguese,&nbsp;
schiavo in Italian, and Sklave in German.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:34.740
Same linguistic change happened in Greek, where&nbsp;
the word sclavos, derived from the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:41.520
Byzantine word for the Slavs, today means slave.&nbsp;
Same thing also briefly happened in Arabic, where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:47.940
during the middle ages, the word Saqaliba could&nbsp;
mean both slavs or white slaves, although this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:52.260
change wasn’t uniform across the arab world&nbsp;
nor did it survive past the middle ages.

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:58.620
The life of a slave during the middle ages was&nbsp;
heavily dependent on where they ended up. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:04.740
for the most part, the kind of slavery present&nbsp;
in 19th century Americas wasn’t really common in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:10.680
the middle ages. As William Phillips states ‘Most&nbsp;
societies had mechanisms for slave acculturation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:16.260
most provided means for them to become free,&nbsp;
and most allowed a lesser or greater degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:22.020
of assimilation into the dominant group.’ This is&nbsp;
not to say that they weren’t treated poorly. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:27.540
most certainly were, after all the survival rate&nbsp;
of castration in mediaeval Europe was quite low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:32.040
however, it is important to give some&nbsp;
point of reference as to the scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:35.700
of the treatment compared to something&nbsp;
that people might be more familiar with.

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:41.880
It is also prudent to mention, if it wasn’t&nbsp;
obvious already, that not all medieval slaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.220
were slavs. The slavs constituted the majority&nbsp;
of the medieval european slave population not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:54.180
its entirety. Also, as Mats Roslund states ‘Slave&nbsp;
traders made no ethnic distinctions when selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:59.940
and buying’. In other words, slavs weren’t more&nbsp;
likely to be enslaved because they were slavs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:05.580
Slavery based on some kind of perceived race&nbsp;
or ethnicity didn’t really exist in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:10.740
ages. Slavs constituted the largest number of&nbsp;
slaves because of their economic, political,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:15.480
geographical, and religious situations&nbsp;
not because of their quote unquote ‘race’.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:21.840
In the 11th and 12th century, when Hungarian,&nbsp;
Polish, Rus and various Nomadic polities where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:27.180
more firmly established in central and eastern&nbsp;
Europe, the slavic slave trade didn’t stop. Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:33.180
among these polities provided a supply of slaves&nbsp;
and lawless areas inbetween them still harbert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:38.640
some pagan Slavs who could be captured and sold.&nbsp;
But past the high middle ages the slavic slave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:44.460
trade did slow down. The primary reason for this&nbsp;
slow down was the full conversion of all the Slavs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:49.620
to christianity. Once christian it became harder&nbsp;
for the Hungarians, the Germans, and various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:55.740
other Slavs to justify the enslavement of other&nbsp;
fellow christians. Plus, the slow expansion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.720
feudalism into eastern europe, and the expansion&nbsp;
of control by the various polities into all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:05.880
lawless areas of eastern europe, meant that slave&nbsp;
raiding became much harder to conduct. This didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:11.640
mean that slavic enslavement went fully away,&nbsp;
trading cities like Genoa and Venice found ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:16.020
around the religious rules, and areas in today's&nbsp;
southern Ukraine and Russia continued to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:21.060
raided for slaves by various Nomadic groups who&nbsp;
where primarily sponsored by the Ottomans. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:26.400
the height of the medieval Slavic slave trade,&nbsp;
the trade that gave us the word slave itself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:32.060
possibly even contributed to the kickstarting&nbsp;
of the Western European economy, was no more.

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:33.060
…

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:39.600
With all that said, some slavs today often react&nbsp;
negatively to the idea that the word slave comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:45.240
from the word slav and sometimes even try&nbsp;
to deny it. This response is not unfounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:50.940
as anti-slavic sentiments had been very strong&nbsp;
in Western Europe for centuries. German scholar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:57.480
Friedrich Schlegel in 1808 described the Slavs&nbsp;
as a ‘shallow Chinese race’, Engels and Marx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.480 --> 00:18:03.780
stated in 1851 that ‘the natural and inevitable&nbsp;
fate of these dying Slavic nations is to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:08.460
this process of dissolution and absorption&nbsp;
by their stronger [Germanic] neighbours to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:14.580
complete itself.’ A British ethnologist Robert&nbsp;
Knox, in 1862, described the ‘barbours slavs’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:19.800
as ‘uncivilised’ and ‘inferior to the Saxon&nbsp;
race’. And, of course, I don’t even need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:25.320
mention the idea of the ‘Untermenschen from the&nbsp;
east’ which was based on 19th century anti-slavic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:31.500
propaganda and was extensively used by the Nazis.&nbsp;
With all this history of racism against the slavs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:36.900
it should not be surprising that some slavs&nbsp;
today are quite defensive, even denialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:42.000
when someone mentions the prominent medieval&nbsp;
slavic slave trade or the fact that the word slave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:48.480
comes from the word slav. Because, after all, it&nbsp;
can seem like yet another racist historical take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:57.120
against the slavs rather than actual history.
However, denying the obvious historical and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:01.260
etymological facts isn’t really going to&nbsp;
help us improve our understanding of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:06.420
past or learn from it for the future. Plus, I&nbsp;
think the slavic slave trade is an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:12.660
part of history that is sadly not talked about&nbsp;
outside of academia. So, hopefully this video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:16.320
introduced you to an interesting part of&nbsp;
history that you didn’t know much about.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.720
Well, this video will not be controversial at&nbsp;
all. If you want to learn more about slavery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:26.520
in the middle ages a good introduction is&nbsp;
the book Slavery After Rome, by Alice Rio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.520 --> 00:19:31.560
and if you want a more complicated academic&nbsp;
deep dive into specifically viking and slavic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:36.180
medieval slavery then I recommend reading&nbsp;
The Archaeology of Slavery in Early Medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:39.840
Northern Europe. Other than that it’s the&nbsp;
holidays so I hope you’re all having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:44.040
good time and maybe considering giving me some&nbsp;
support whether on Patreon or here on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:48.480
it really does help and makes a difference&nbsp;
and is tremendously appreciated. This fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.320
I was sick for a month and the financial&nbsp;
support really did help a lot so thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:58.020
to all my supporters. And with that, my name&nbsp;
is Matus Laser, and stick around, for history.

